1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:03,700
Football Intelligence Platform — From match video to validated football intelligence

2
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:06,875
Clubs generate huge amounts of match video.

3
00:00:06,875 --> 00:00:11,394
But turning that footage into reliable, structured information is still hard.

4
00:00:11,394 --> 00:00:13,858
This is a football intelligence platform:

5
00:00:13,858 --> 00:00:18,377
a pipeline that turns raw match video into validated football data,

6
00:00:18,377 --> 00:00:21,252
and eventually, intelligence you can act on.

7
00:00:21,252 --> 00:00:25,360
Here's the current review workflow, running on real match footage.

8
00:00:25,360 --> 00:00:31,418
We don't ask an AI model to watch a ninety minute match and guess what happened.

9
00:00:31,418 --> 00:00:34,068
Instead, the platform divides video into controlled,

10
00:00:34,068 --> 00:00:37,854
thirty to sixty second segments, small enough to trace, test,

11
00:00:37,854 --> 00:00:41,262
and validate independently. Many segments get reviewed over time.

12
00:00:41,262 --> 00:00:44,291
Not to train the engine on manual clicks,

13
00:00:44,291 --> 00:00:47,698
but to expose where its football logic is weak.

14
00:00:47,698 --> 00:00:51,106
Manual labels stay strictly evaluation only: never inference inputs,

15
00:00:51,106 --> 00:00:52,620
never a timestamp exception.

16
00:00:52,620 --> 00:00:57,012
Here is the segment builder inside the Football Event Review workflow,

17
00:00:57,012 --> 00:01:01,403
running against real Alfheim match footage. An operator prepares a segment,

18
00:01:01,403 --> 00:01:05,795
this one, fifteen to sixteen minutes in. The platform extracts frames,

19
00:01:05,795 --> 00:01:09,787
caches detections, and lays out video, tracking, evidence, and review,

20
00:01:09,787 --> 00:01:12,582
side by side. Segment length is configurable,

21
00:01:12,582 --> 00:01:14,578
typically thirty or sixty seconds,

22
00:01:14,578 --> 00:01:18,970
so every result traces back to an exact slice of video.

23
00:01:18,970 --> 00:01:22,636
Inside each segment, computer vision detects players, the ball,

24
00:01:22,636 --> 00:01:27,116
and team shape, and proposes candidate events. Passes, turnovers, shots, fouls,

25
00:01:27,116 --> 00:01:31,190
each with a timestamp, a team, and a confidence score.

26
00:01:31,190 --> 00:01:35,263
A raw AI prediction is a proposal, not a fact.

27
00:01:35,263 --> 00:01:41,373
Every candidate is independently reconstructed from the video before it's compared to the engine's output,

28
00:01:41,373 --> 00:01:45,853
and only then does it earn its place in the statistics.

29
00:01:45,853 --> 00:01:47,890
Predictions get questioned, never assumed.

30
00:01:47,890 --> 00:01:50,682
This is the Global Football Rules Reviewer.

31
00:01:50,682 --> 00:01:55,866
Every candidate event is checked against the actual I-FAB Laws of the Game,

32
00:01:55,866 --> 00:01:58,259
and the project's own analytics definitions.

33
00:01:58,259 --> 00:02:00,652
Then independently reconstructed from the video,

34
00:02:00,652 --> 00:02:03,443
before it's compared to the engine's output.

35
00:02:03,443 --> 00:02:07,830
In this fifteen to sixteen minute segment, fifteen proposals were reviewed.

36
00:02:07,830 --> 00:02:11,818
Fourteen matched confirmed engine events exactly. Most lined up directly,

37
00:02:11,818 --> 00:02:17,401
and one engine event was independently verified after its proposal didn't correspond to it.

38
00:02:17,401 --> 00:02:24,181
The one remaining proposal that didn't match a distinct engine event was excluded from the published statistics.

39
00:02:24,181 --> 00:02:28,169
And match state annotations stayed out of the numbers too.

40
00:02:28,169 --> 00:02:31,758
All one hundred and five protected regression tests passed,

41
00:02:31,758 --> 00:02:34,550
before this segment was published and locked.

42
00:02:34,550 --> 00:02:39,665
Only validated events feed the statistics layer. Possession, passes, pass completion,

43
00:02:39,665 --> 00:02:43,850
fouls, and interceptions are implemented and undergoing thorough validation.

44
00:02:43,850 --> 00:02:48,035
Shots and shots on target are currently in review.

45
00:02:48,035 --> 00:02:53,150
Corners are near completion. Offsides, attacking and defensive actions, and player,

46
00:02:53,150 --> 00:02:56,870
team, and formation statistics are still in development.

47
00:02:56,870 --> 00:03:00,590
We'd rather show exactly where each statistic stands,

48
00:03:00,590 --> 00:03:02,450
than call everything finished.

49
00:03:02,450 --> 00:03:06,329
Once data is validated, Copilot can reason over it directly.

50
00:03:06,329 --> 00:03:09,433
How many passes completed, how sure we are,

51
00:03:09,433 --> 00:03:10,984
what still needs review.

52
00:03:10,984 --> 00:03:15,251
Because it answers from validated structured events rather than raw predictions,

53
00:03:15,251 --> 00:03:19,518
the insight is only as reliable as the validation behind it.

54
00:03:19,518 --> 00:03:21,070
That's exactly the point.

55
00:03:21,070 --> 00:03:24,618
The same architecture can extend to tactical patterns, scouting,

56
00:03:24,618 --> 00:03:28,561
and player development. Including heat maps and pitch zone views,

57
00:03:28,561 --> 00:03:32,898
built from the full recorded clip. Further ahead, the same segmented,

58
00:03:32,898 --> 00:03:36,841
state machine driven review is designed to eventually run continuously.

59
00:03:36,841 --> 00:03:39,206
Configurable thirty or sixty second chunks,

60
00:03:39,206 --> 00:03:42,361
feeding the same law grounded match state engine,

61
00:03:42,361 --> 00:03:47,486
appending validated statistics to the screen as more of a match is processed.

62
00:03:47,486 --> 00:03:51,429
The rules engine itself could then refresh on a regular,

63
00:03:51,429 --> 00:03:54,977
even daily, cadence. Always through the same evidence based,

64
00:03:54,977 --> 00:03:56,160
regression protected process.

65
00:03:56,160 --> 00:04:00,465
One further concept worth naming on its own: Query By Probability.

66
00:04:00,465 --> 00:04:03,988
One strong primary camera handles the main stream continuously.

67
00:04:03,988 --> 00:04:06,727
Only when its own detection confidence drops,

68
00:04:06,727 --> 00:04:09,467
for particular frames or a short window,

69
00:04:09,467 --> 00:04:14,555
does the system query the specific secondary camera that actually covers that moment.

70
00:04:14,555 --> 00:04:16,903
A goal end or touchline camera,

71
00:04:16,903 --> 00:04:20,426
fusing its evidence back in to strengthen the decision,

72
00:04:20,426 --> 00:04:22,774
then reverting to the main view.

73
00:04:22,774 --> 00:04:26,688
It never watches every secondary stream for the whole match.

74
00:04:26,688 --> 00:04:30,602
This is architecture we're exploring for larger, multi camera venues.

75
00:04:30,602 --> 00:04:32,950
A future concept, not current behaviour.

76
00:04:32,950 --> 00:04:35,775
This prototype was built by a focused team,

77
00:04:35,775 --> 00:04:38,247
to prove the concept quickly and honestly.

78
00:04:38,247 --> 00:04:41,426
Showing each statistic at its real stage of validation,

79
00:04:41,426 --> 00:04:43,544
rather than dressed up as finished.

80
00:04:43,544 --> 00:04:47,429
If you want to take it further, Xebia Netherlands, shown here,

81
00:04:47,429 --> 00:04:49,901
can bring additional AI and engineering capacity,

82
00:04:49,901 --> 00:04:52,020
to help scale the next phase.

83
00:04:52,320 --> 00:04:57,720
Xebia Netherlands — scalable AI & engineering capacity for a next phase

